---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import LessonIllustration from "../../components/LessonIllustration.astro";
import MapStructureDemo from "../../components/MapStructureDemo.astro";
import StructureFacts from "../../components/StructureFacts.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import { orMapTerm } from "../../lib/atlas/standalone-terms";
import "../../styles/set-lesson.css";
---

<BaseLayout title="OR-map" description="Alice crosses out a stockpile entry while Bob records a new crate delivery." territory="Structures" sheetTitle="OR-map">
  <article class="register-lesson">
    <header class="page-intro"><p class="territory-label">Structures</p><h1>OR-map</h1><p class="structure-summary">Alice, Bob, and Carol each have a copy of the Eagle Creek stockpile list. Alice crosses out the old listing while Bob records a new delivery. Neither has seen the other's note.</p><LessonIllustration id="structures-or-map" /></header>
    <StructureFacts structure="OR-map" family="CRDT" model="Observed-remove map with nested CRDT values" updates="Change a tally or remove a named entry" merge="Remove what was seen; keep unseen changes made at the same time." delivery="Safe under repeated and out-of-order delivery" bestFor="Named ledgers that retain unseen updates after a removal" cost="Entry identities, tally state, and a record of removals" />
    <section aria-labelledby="identity-title">
      <h2 id="identity-title">Cross out only what you have seen</h2>
      <p>
        All three hikers start with <code>Eagle Creek · 5 crates</code> in
        their notebooks. Alice thinks the old stockpile listing is no longer
        needed, so she crosses out her line. She has not removed any physical
        crates. Bob has not seen her note. He records a delivery of 3 more
        crates and changes his own total from 5 to 8. Carol still has the
        earlier 5-crate report.
      </p>
      <p>
        An <dfn><a href="/glossary/#or-map">OR-map</a></dfn> is an
        observed-remove map: Alice can remove the entry she saw, but she
        cannot remove Bob's update before she receives it.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...orMapTerm} />
      <figure class="notebook-comparison notebook-comparison-wide" tabindex="0">
        <table aria-label="OR-map notebooks at three checkpoints">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Hiker</th>
              <th scope="col">Before either note</th>
              <th scope="col">Notes not yet shared</th>
              <th scope="col">After sharing</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Alice</th>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 5 crates</td>
              <td><del>Eagle Creek · 5 crates</del></td>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 8 crates</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Bob</th>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 5 crates</td>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 8 crates (5 + 3)</td>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 8 crates</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Carol</th>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 5 crates</td>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 5 crates</td>
              <td>Eagle Creek · 8 crates</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>
          Before sharing, Bob's update has not reached Alice, and Carol has
          received neither note.
          <span class="notebook-scroll-hint">Scroll sideways to read every notebook.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        When the hikers exchange notes, they keep the Eagle Creek line
        because Alice had not seen Bob's update when she crossed it out.
        Bob's tally includes the original 5 crates and the 3 he added, so
        all three notebooks show 8. If Alice had seen Bob's update before
        crossing out the line, she could have removed that entry too.
      </p>
    </section>
    <MapStructureDemo kind="or-map" title="Keep a new stockpile note" description="Start with 5 crates. Alice crosses out the line she saw while Bob logs 3 more. Compare their notebooks." rule="Crossing out an entry does not remove a new note you have not seen." raceLabel="Race removal against the 3-crate delivery" />
    <section aria-labelledby="modes-title">
      <h2 id="modes-title">A list of names, with more inside</h2>
      <p>
        Alice, Bob, and Carol have two things to settle: whether the Eagle
        Creek line remains, and how many crates to record on it. They compare
        the notes behind the line to settle its removal, then combine Bob's
        delivery with the tally already there. The demo uses tallies, but
        Watershed OR-maps can instead hold dated answers, competing answers,
        or lists of text. The hikers use one of these kinds throughout a
        given notebook.
      </p>
      <p>
        An OR-map gives each named line a structure with its own rule for
        combining changes. That makes it one of the most flexible structures
        here: the hikers can cross out a line without discarding an unseen
        change to its contents.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="source-title">
      <h2 id="source-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Sources:</strong> Watershed's{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/or_map_kernel.gleam">OR-map kernel</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Implementation:</strong> The demo calls the kernel's tally mode
        through the Gleam toolkit to merge the three clients' stockpile updates.
      </p>
    </section>
    <nav class="structure-next" aria-label="Continue through map structures"><a href="/structures/lww-map/">Back to LWWMap</a><a href="/structures/maps/">Browse map family</a><a href="/structures/shared-directory/">Continue to SharedDirectory</a></nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
